# Vercel Speed Insights Implementation

## Summary
Successfully implemented Vercel Speed Insights for the Mon-Plant React application following the official Vercel documentation.

## Changes Made

### 1. Installed Dependencies
- Added `@vercel/speed-insights` package to the project
- Updated `package.json` with the new dependency
- Updated `package-lock.json` with the resolved dependency tree

### 2. Modified Source Files
**frontend/src/App.tsx**
- Imported `SpeedInsights` component from `@vercel/speed-insights/react`
- Added `<SpeedInsights />` component to the App component
- Wrapped the existing `<AppRoutes />` in a React Fragment to accommodate the new component

## Implementation Details

The implementation follows the standard React integration pattern as documented in the Vercel Speed Insights guide:
- Used the `/react` import path which is appropriate for Create React App and Vite-based React applications
- Placed the component at the root level of the App to ensure it tracks all routes
- The component is now rendered alongside the application routes, allowing it to monitor performance metrics across the entire application

## Testing

- ✅ Build completed successfully with no errors
- ✅ Linting shows only pre-existing issues unrelated to this change
- ✅ The TypeScript compilation passed without errors

## Next Steps

To complete the Speed Insights setup:
1. Enable Speed Insights in the Vercel dashboard for this project
2. Deploy the application to Vercel
3. After deployment, verify the `/_vercel/speed-insights/script.js` script is loaded
4. View performance metrics in the Vercel dashboard under the Speed Insights tab

## Technical Notes

- This is a React + Vite + TypeScript project using npm as the package manager
- The application uses React Router for routing, which is compatible with Speed Insights
- The Speed Insights component will automatically track Core Web Vitals and other performance metrics once deployed to Vercel
